and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a}$, we multiply each of the components by $-5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot a &= -5(2i + 3j) \\\\\n&= (-5 \\cdot 2)i + (-5 \\cdot 3)j \\\\\n&=-10i + - 15j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186060907 1064 "Suppose we are given two vectors \n$\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle,$ shown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$  and  $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}?$\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that $\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =(2i+3j)+(-4i+4j) \\\\  & =(2+(-4))i+(3+4)j \\\\  & =-2i+7j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n![](images/ea/eae25a74dde73f7899f6ccbbdbb1a1f589fc72cad9629a33b4fdbbbc753efcd8.svg)\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a},$ we multiply each of the components by $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot a & =-5(2i+3j) \\\\  & =(-5\\cdot 2)i+(-5\\cdot 3)j \\\\  & =-10i+-15j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get \n![](images/05/05f847a73201db3de20db3847ef4ed97e07ea7fae9325401b1ea5a460968f9da.svg)" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186060908 1064 "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨2, 5⟩ + ⟨- 3, 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨2 + (-3), 5 + 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 1, 12⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 + (-3) \\\\ 5 + 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot ⟨2, 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨(-5) \\cdot 2, (-5) \\cdot 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 10,-25⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(-5) \\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5) \\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186060908 1064 "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2,5⟩+⟨-3,7⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨2+(-3),5+7⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨-1,12⟩ & ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+(-3) \\\\ 5+7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot ⟨2,5⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨(-5)\\cdot 2,(-5)\\cdot 5⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨-10,-25⟩ & ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186060909 1064 "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v}$, or\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}$.\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n$\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}$.\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v}$, is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186060909 1064 "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v},$ or\n\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}.$\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n\n$$\n\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}.\n$$\n\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v},$ is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186060910 1064 "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a}$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA$.\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}XA^(→) & &= \\end{vmatrix}4|= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{\\begin{vmatrix}OX^(→) & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}XA^(→)\\right|^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186060910 1064 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nExpressed in component form, our vector is $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i} + 4 \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nTo find the magnitude (length) of $\\mathbf{a},$ we can use the Pythagorean theorem on $\\triangle OXA.$\n\nThe legs of the triangle have lengths\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{OX}| & =|-2|=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{XA}| & =|\\,4\\,|=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{|\\overset{\\to }{OX}{|}^{2}+|\\overset{\\to }{XA}{|}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186060911 1064 "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nwas given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a_{x} \\\\ a_{y}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186060911 1064 "We just saw that the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4 \\rangle$ was given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a vector\n![](images/10/10fbddb6dc3528629e5583ad25976d049ca2ec5943ce3e689953f79299b8afcd.svg)\nthe magnitude of the vector can always be computed as\n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| = \\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nWe sometimes use the notation $|| \\mathbf a ||$ to denote the magnitude of a vector." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186064239 1038 17592186573272 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186064310 1038 17592186573289 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186068719 1038 17592186573302 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186078407 1038 17592186573260 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186096654 1038 17592186573252 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186096836 1038 17592186573290 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100827 1038 17592186573257 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100827 1038 17592186573282 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100854 1038 17592186573291 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100854 1038 17592186573322 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100855 1038 17592186573269 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100855 1038 17592186573299 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100856 1038 17592186573254 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100856 1038 17592186573286 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100856 1038 17592186573321 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100919 1038 17592186573265 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100919 1038 17592186573280 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 17592186573263 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 17592186573314 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186101008 1038 17592186573278 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186101032 1050 420.0 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186101946 1064 "We can use Cartesian coordinates to define vectors in the plane. For example, consider the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad622207f396145e930cf74f7905104bf12fa394a9f2f4f5d7ce761cc8e36.png)\n\nThe point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3)$. We also have the point $O(0,0)$ that is called the **origin**.\n\nThe vector\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nis called the **position vector** of $A$. A position vector always has its tail at the origin.\n\nNow let's define two special unit vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\, \\mathbf{i} \\, & = & \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\end{vmatrix} = 1\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n- $\\mathbf{i}$ is a horizontal unit vector that points to the right along the positive direction of the $x$-axis, while\n- $\\mathbf{j}$ is a vertical unit vector that points upwards along the positive direction of the $y$-axis.\n\nThese vectors are shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb5694945b6f76f14a4e66b854f5daa56f3e607bd5b24c4f361ed844dbf8ff7e.png)\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nOX^(→) = 2i,; XA^(→) = OY^(→) = 3j\n$$\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOA^(→) &= OX^(→) + XA^(→) \\\\\n&= 2i + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $A$ with coordinates $(2,3)$ has a position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOA^(→) &= 2i + 3j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y)$, then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a} = a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\mathbf{j}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186101946 1064 "We can use Cartesian coordinates to define vectors in the plane. For example, consider the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad622207f396145e930cf74f7905104bf12fa394a9f2f4f5d7ce761cc8e36.png)\n\nThe point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3).$ We also have the point $O(0,0)$ that is called the **origin**. \n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ is called the **position vector** of $A.$ A position vector always has its tail at the origin. \n\nNow let's define two special unit vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ where $|\\, \\mathbf{i} \\,| = |\\, \\mathbf{j} \\,| = 1,$ as follows:\n\n- $\\mathbf{i}$ is a horizontal unit vector that points to the right along the positive direction of the $x$-axis, while\n\n- $\\mathbf{j}$ is a vertical unit vector that points upwards along the positive direction of the $y$-axis.\n\nThese vectors are shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb5694945b6f76f14a4e66b854f5daa56f3e607bd5b24c4f361ed844dbf8ff7e.png)\n\nWe have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OX}=2i, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{XA}=\\overset{\\to }{OY}=3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XA} \\\\  & =2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $A$ with coordinates $(2,3)$ has a position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $(a_x,a_y),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a} = a_x\\mathbf{i}+a_y\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186101947 1064 "The $\\mathbf{i,j}$ notation can sometimes be cumbersome. An alternative is to use **angle bracket** notation. For example, using angle bracket notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}=\\langle 2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNote that, using angle bracket notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as\n\n$$\ni = 1i + 0j = ⟨1, 0⟩,; j = 0i + 1j = ⟨0, 1⟩\n$$\n\nWe can also use **column vector** notation, which is similar to angle bracket notation but expresses the vector as a column rather than a row. For example, using column vector notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing column vector notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** Sometimes, the round bracket notation $(\\ldots)$ is used to denote vectors. However, we will avoid using that notation here, because it also looks like the coordinates of a point. A point and its position vector are two separate objects!" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186101947 1064 "The $\\mathbf{i,j}$ notation can sometimes be cumbersome. An alternative is to use **angle bracket** notation. For example, using angle bracket notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}=\\langle 2,3 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNote that, using angle bracket notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i &=1i+0j=⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ j &=0i+1j=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use **column vector** notation, which is similar to angle bracket notation but expresses the vector as a column rather than a row. For example, using column vector notation, the vector $2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ can be expressed as\n\n![](images/24/2419e5c69bef2cc15f00106e4bf83a17ab2277ed5ea43e8b17f904ca6318873f.svg)\n\nUsing column vector notation, the special unit vectors can be expressed as ![](images/f5/f51854ec5bb92b38e5078cfe96046535f8e8efecf1304ea5cc4605dddec09573.svg) and ![](images/ff/ff6013f4f80815a7ca97b5ba8929c2d39b2a4e0165cbb5a8534d8ffafc4377e8.svg)\n\n**Note:** Sometimes, the round bracket notation $(\\ldots)$ is used to denote vectors. However, we will avoid using that notation here, because it also looks like the coordinates of a point. A point and its position vector are two separate objects!" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186101948 1064 "If $A$ and $B$ are points in the Cartesian plane with position vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, then\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nTo understand why the equation above is true, remember that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, given $A(5,13)$ and $B(-2,-35)$, we have\n\n$$\nOA^(→) = a = 5i + 13j,; OB^(→) = b =-2i - 35j\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= (-2i - 35j) - (5i + 13j) \\\\\n&=-7i - 48j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 false] [17592186101948 1064 "If $A$ and $B$ are points in the Cartesian plane with position vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, then\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nTo understand why the equation above is true, remember that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, given $A(5,13)$ and $B(-2,-35)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=5i+13j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-2i-35j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i-35j)-(5i+13j) \\\\  & =-7i-48j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186105740 1038 17592186573255 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186105871 1038 17592186573258 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186115249 1038 17592186573266 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186115250 1038 17592186573259 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186115250 1038 17592186573325 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186115251 1038 17592186573270 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186115251 1038 17592186573313 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186119778 1038 17592186573271 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186119778 1038 17592186573279 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186119778 1038 17592186573296 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186119779 1038 17592186573277 13194139536444 17592186045417 t