194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573247 1078 17592186572861 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573247 1079 #uuid "f71eec51-55f5-5ed3-a5a6-c93f3bce746d" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1075 17592186572858 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1075 17592186572946 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1075 17592186573074 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1075 17592186573078 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1075 17592186573095 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1076 17592186045493 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1077 "selection" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1078 17592186573074 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573248 1079 #uuid "fe46b4b1-2f2f-5fb3-9e1d-afbfc19fa446" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1075 17592186572880 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1075 17592186572931 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1075 17592186573006 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1075 17592186573100 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1075 17592186573137 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1076 17592186045493 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1077 "selection" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1078 17592186572931 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573249 1079 #uuid "ff468151-5610-57e8-a342-0015e9de243b" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1068 17592186573185 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1069 "q-152871" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi )=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1072 #uuid "002cab17-2b93-5a94-bc24-04ae7b92f4f9" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573250 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi :$   \n\n![](images/42/42eb38a11d9d63e05c0078bae4c052566b3162571e8563f22cd52ae31bc6dbd7.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=4$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1068 17592186573205 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1069 "q-80840" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1071 "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zx}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,{f}_{yz}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z)+{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1072 #uuid "0032ce9b-c24d-5a4a-8814-0479ffd35234" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573251 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{yzy}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{yz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxx}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zx}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z)+{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}+\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(1,2,1)+{f}_{xxz}(1,2,1)=0.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1068 17592186573191 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1069 "q-11749" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1071 "![](images/e0/e0caa2bd7ac6b620ec267d3cc50493701af1f2d6718ddcb090c71f408d2a82ab.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\text{b}$ and $\\text{c}$ are shown in the diagram above. Which of the following could be $-b+c.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1072 #uuid "06291cf0-d6c5-5a7a-a5c7-3922a0916c3b" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573252 1074 "We interpret  $-b+c$ as the addition\n\n$$\n(-b)+c,\n$$\n\nwhere $-b$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $b$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $b.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $-b$ and $c:$ \n\n- We can start $-b$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $c$ and has its tail placed at the head of $-b.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $-\\text{b}$ to the head of $\\text{c}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/873334db27c4cf2d85d262d98050941307026fb7b0341d5c871a5ad8eb565313.png)" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1068 17592186573226 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1069 "q-30824" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1071 "${log}_{64}⁡(\\sqrt{8})=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1072 #uuid "070008c9-e913-5456-a5af-e255c5aba081" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573253 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=64$ and $X=\\sqrt{8}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $8,$ we set $b=8.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{64}⁡(\\sqrt{8}) & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}{{log}_{8}⁡(64)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{1/2})}{{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1068 17592186573240 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1069 "q-124990" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1071 "![](images/cc/ccb8643e7dfde7aa26d290c5f6e49a2037f37594bc05c558c46dea6bd673d9ff.png)\n\nLet $A(1,1)$ and $B(3,2).$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ in terms of $i$ and $j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1072 #uuid "092e8310-501d-5972-983c-e6366efca630" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573254 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i+2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =2i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1068 17592186573215 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1069 "q-171894" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1071 "![](images/71/71434714d4941799da3859979b9a75950994efef09b523da00a6a11523e3b906.png)\n\nGiven that the triangle $△ABC$ shown above is isosceles, the measure of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ must be equal to{{field-1}}." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1072 #uuid "0a6c019b-459a-54b3-94ee-3cfc489e4bfd" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1074 "Since $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{CB}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =CB \\\\ 3x-9 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =3x-9 \\\\  & =3(8)-9 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1068 17592186573194 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1069 "q-338264" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1072 #uuid "0ac40a4c-aba2-5c77-8ace-67895a16cfed" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573256 1074 "Notice that as $n\\to 0,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}=e\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n} & ={lim}_{n\\to 0}{[{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={e}^{4/7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1068 17592186573154 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1069 "q-48724" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(-2)=-3,\\,g(-2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(-2)=6\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=-2.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1072 #uuid "0b93f5e4-441e-52cb-bcd1-657f8b3f7ca5" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573257 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=-2} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)g(-2)-f(-2){g}^{′}(-2)}{{g}^{2}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 6}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1068 17592186573235 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1069 "q-87850" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1071 "![](images/26/26ab73d81c0ec3ef9b2b6fa93de11266fb4d0629b875d7d38636f36926f0f053.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1072 #uuid "0be768b0-d70e-57fe-9f86-a4cf5ff73d92" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573258 1074 "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right triangle. \n\n![](images/e1/e13a301fe37d44657754877ef61b4d5e0e913c309784baa50d53f0d0e5edd913.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1068 17592186573201 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1069 "q-31944" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1071 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $P$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${P}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[P\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1072 #uuid "0c4fe725-e7cf-5733-870d-da1a638a1a59" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573259 1074 "We write down $P$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $P$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[P\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1068 17592186573182 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1069 "q-339011" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1071 "Find the value of $a$ given that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1072 #uuid "0c781737-7cc2-58de-b406-ca857cb292ed" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573260 1074 "First, we let \n\n$$\nL=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=L.\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(a).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand so,  $L=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $L=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =4 \\\\ a & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1068 17592186573150 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1069 "q-73637" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1072 #uuid "0dd8f23a-a284-5af7-a370-b6877f8aea89" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573261 1074 "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1068 17592186573162 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1068 17592186573188 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1069 "q-318003" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1071 "If $a=3i-5j$ and $b=-4i+7j,$ then $a-b=${{field-1}} $i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}} $j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1072 #uuid "0e7fd5bf-cb04-57ce-b6b4-49eeaabcc873" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573262 1074 "To compute $a-b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b & =(3i-5j)-(-4i+7j) \\\\  & =3i-5j+4i-7j \\\\  & =(3i+4i)+(-5j-7j) \\\\  & =7i-12j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1068 17592186573179 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1069 "q-104443" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(0)=5,\\,{f}^{′}(0)=-2,\\,g(0)=10,\\,{g}^{′}(0)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=0.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1072 #uuid "0f8b5f6f-ca62-5fd3-b5bd-ae649230f08c" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573263 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=0} & =f(0){g}^{′}(0)+g(0){f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+10\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1068 17592186573149 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1068 17592186573208 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1069 "q-318122" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1071 "If $a=⟨2,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2⟩$, then $a-b=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1072 #uuid "149252a8-4a85-5fa3-b726-083b4624a5d8" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573264 1074 "To compute $a-b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b & =⟨2,-1⟩-⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-(-1),-1-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1068 17592186573192 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1069 "q-72999" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1071 "For the functions $g(x)$ and $u(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\ng(-2)=-6,\\,{g}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,u(-2)=4,\\,{u}^{′}(-2)=-8\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2g(x)+u(x))$ at $x=-2.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1072 #uuid "26a28da3-48f6-5069-8f6b-b7cd82087ac3" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573265 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2g(x)+u(x))=2{g}^{′}(x)+{u}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2g(x)+u(x))|}_{x=-2} & =2{g}^{′}(-2)+{u}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+(-8) \\\\  & =6-8 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1068 17592186573168 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1069 "q-31873" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1072 #uuid "2b6dc055-10e0-54e5-be96-be9101fa62fe" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573266 1074 "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1068 17592186573213 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1069 "q-66885" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1071 "${log}_{64}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{4})=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1072 #uuid "2e637b5f-6f34-5a1c-b2e1-f9ed9e9b050b" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573267 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=64$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{4}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $4,$ we set $b=4.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{64}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{4}) & =\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{4})}{{log}_{4}⁡(64)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{1/3})}{{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1068 17592186573180 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1069 "q-344566" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1072 #uuid "2f397079-49a9-58d1-bc93-fa23602eefa7" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573268 1074 "![](images/86/86c0cf22de78e3b773543680c2ace077f97b66ae822436d637c5444903e2330f.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)) & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1068 17592186573236 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1069 "q-17993" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1071 "![](images/a8/a86e5e973e15230943beaa3f8afdeed139690ba4e5cf3fed4c35895e5f4bd2d4.png)\n\nThe point $W$ is shown in the picture above. Find the coordinates of the corresponding position vector." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1072 #uuid "33670b3f-b9d2-5fe2-ab57-0eab9223a358" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1074 "For a point with coordinates $({a}_{x},{a}_{y}),$ the position vector is $⟨{a}_{x},{a}_{y}⟩.$\n\nThe point $W$ has coordinates $(1.5,-2.5),$ so the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=⟨1.5,-2.5⟩.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1068 17592186573173 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1069 "q-131369" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1072 #uuid "402decfa-8c35-5ba9-99e4-cbb1a7d10a25" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573270 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -24 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -24 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1068 17592186573164 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1069 "q-88286" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1071 "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1072 #uuid "40a39262-0d1b-5421-8009-5952de54d116" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573271 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 2(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-2)\\cdot 2(1+2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1068 17592186573245 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1069 "q-156126" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1071 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}p-2>3 \\\\ 1-2p\\le -7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1072 #uuid "46ac0cd5-cca2-5b10-8e71-7bc6828cb573" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573272 1074 "To solve the system, we need to find the values of $p$ that satisfy both $p-2>3$ and $1-2p\\le -7$ simultaneously.\n\n- First, we solve $p-2>3\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p-2 & >3 \\\\ p-2+2 & >3+2 \\\\ p & >5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/1189f49b694afcfef7f10a19b523ebbcce3bb8ab13b69e60ebfd5a6c342ff10e.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve $1-2p\\le -7\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2p & \\le -7 \\\\ 1-2p-1 & \\le -7-1 \\\\ -2p & \\le -8 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2p) & \\ge (-1)\\cdot (-8) \\\\ 2p & \\ge 8 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & \\ge \\frac{8}{2} \\\\ p & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8bf3ae94c8048dcf9060c985300f9a808ae73158eeeef6e0f01f3f922d33ea9.png)\n\nTo find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](images/1a/1a4cd0c86983a39354eef09ad60d44f3815c082f443a3eb12bd14f6232988f65.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThus, the final solution is $p\\in (5,\\infty ).$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1068 17592186573183 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1069 "q-182513" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1071 "$\\,$ If you don't submit your assignment, then you will get a zero.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1072 #uuid "4ffccd8c-fd28-5be4-b193-a3d833fff80a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573273 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't submit your assignment.\n\n- $B:$ You will get a zero.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You submit your assignment.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You submit your assignment, or you will get a zero.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Submit your assignment, or you will get a zero." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1068 17592186573248 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1069 "q-6423" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1071 "A triangle has sides $m$, $n$, and $o.$ The sides $m$, $n$, and $o$ are of lengths  $2$, $5$, and $6$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle between the sides $n$ and $o?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1072 #uuid "5021d5eb-c519-52ae-b30c-1049345fdc26" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573274 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/26/26f2041aec84bb0086e2f08c2ba3a19331895951782b7fda153858643a8a5dd4.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $M:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{o}^{2}+{n}^{2}-{m}^{2}}{2no}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm=2,\\,n=5,\\,o=6.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡M & =\\frac{{6}^{2}+{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{2(5)(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{36+25-4}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{57}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{57}{60}) \\\\  & ={18.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1068 17592186573184 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1068 17592186573203 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1069 "q-318151" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1071 "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $2b+(-3a+c).$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$2b+(-3a+c)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1072 #uuid "5042d60d-1d74-5a97-ae4c-6f45b8b23e98" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573275 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2b+(-3a+c) & =2\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1068 17592186573174 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1069 "q-70415" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1071 "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $s=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $3r+2s$ and points the same direction." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1072 #uuid "54931a2c-0179-55ba-b42e-021e69939399" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573276 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction., we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{3r+2s}{|3r+2s|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3r+2s & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-6)+0 \\\\ 3+(-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3r+2s| & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $3r+2s$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{3r+2s}{|3r+2s|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}(3r+2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1068 17592186573195 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1069 "q-42997" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1071 "Let $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ be square matrices. If $A=B\\cdot C,$  $|B|=9$ and $|{C}^{-1}|=-3$, find $|A|.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1072 #uuid "59d8b4d6-365d-545b-a80d-dc97dcfbcac8" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573277 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nAlso, given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({M}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(M)}.\n$$\n\nSince $|C|=\\frac{1}{|{C}^{-1}|}$, we get $|C|=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|B|\\cdot |C| \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1068 17592186573217 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1069 "q-316642" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1071 "![](images/ad/ad5ee5c0c388e0d2a800765288f20350a8332888a54b4eb3fafd5986d6355838.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-2,-1).$ What is the equation of the line?" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1072 #uuid "5a315de4-d76f-5902-ac75-57586afb2f01" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573278 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(-2,-1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-3}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=3$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1068 17592186573220 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1069 "q-42576" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1071 "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1072 #uuid "5dad7293-4ccc-5f12-9124-fcdf29a624d2" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573279 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(4\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5)\\cdot (1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-40)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1068 17592186573171 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1069 "q-311278" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1071 "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=2.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1072 #uuid "61cd2eda-8e2b-568d-83db-33ce3428cd1f" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573280 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))=3{q}^{′}(x)+4{r}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=2} & =3{q}^{′}(2)+4{r}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =3(-3)+4(2) \\\\  & =-9+8 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1068 17592186573176 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1068 17592186573181 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1069 "q-175584" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1071 "$\\frac{7}{2}u+4w-\\frac{2}{3}u-w=${{field-1}}\n$u\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$w$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1072 #uuid "64689025-90b1-5416-8d71-9bc463abeb1c" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573281 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}u+4w-\\frac{2}{3}u-w & = \\\\ (\\frac{7}{2}u-\\frac{2}{3}u)+(4w-w) & = \\\\ (\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{2}{3})u+(4-1)w & = \\\\ (\\frac{21}{6}-\\frac{4}{6})u+3w & = \\\\ (\\frac{21-4}{6})u+3w & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\,u+3\\,w\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1068 17592186573216 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1069 "q-104450" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1071 "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-4)=2e,\\,{f}^{′}(-4)=-e,\\,g(-4)=-3e,\\,{g}^{′}(-4)=e\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=-4.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1072 #uuid "65401c01-5232-5bed-88ce-91552174b310" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573282 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-4,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=-4} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-4)g(-4)-f(-4){g}^{′}(-4)}{{g}^{2}(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-e\\cdot (-3e)-2e\\cdot e}{9{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1068 17592186573159 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1069 "q-320965" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1071 "![](images/45/453e8fc1c4d8a5262fd0a8aa7341dd71b909a4a72cec94cd2698f800f7dc06c9.png)\n\nLet $A(0,3)$ and $B(4,-1).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1072 #uuid "65c77d8f-4e68-5667-bebf-382cd66bf3e2" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=4i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i-j)-(0i+3j) \\\\  & =4i-4j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1068 17592186573170 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1069 "q-1390" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1071 "If $\\overset{\\to }{c}=⟨12,-18⟩$, then $-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c}=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1072 #uuid "71659674-1123-5b9b-95e7-0ead2ac9950c" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1074 "To compute $-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨12,-18⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 12,(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (-18)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1068 17592186573225 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1069 "q-80650" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =a{y}^{3}{e}^{x-1}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{3}{e}^{y-1}+3b{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}, \\\\ {f}_{x}(1,1) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1072 #uuid "71adc372-d2f3-5857-a2ad-a0dc4396f12a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573285 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{y}^{3}{e}^{x-1}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1}) \\\\  & =3a{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{e}^{y-1}+3b{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1}+3b{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1} &=3{x}^{2}{e}^{y-1}+3b{y}^{2}{e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${y}^{2}{e}^{x-1},$ respectively, we see that $a=b.$ \n\nSince ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4 \\\\ a(1)^{3}{e}^{0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{0} & =4 \\\\ a+3 & =4 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=a=1.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1068 17592186573200 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1069 "q-18000" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1071 "![](images/84/84d2c65b53d4095bc3501f9658f2ff597dd9b72fff6e5e2b5b7347834632dfac.png)\n\nLet $A(2,0)$ and $B(-3,-2).$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ in terms of $i$ and $j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1072 #uuid "72c863fb-10f9-5099-ad8d-1290ffd2a6a9" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2i+0j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-3i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(2i+0j) \\\\  & =-5i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1068 17592186573161 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1069 "q-1307" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1071 "If $u=⟨2,3⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $u?$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1072 #uuid "84731c4b-0fa0-51b7-ab58-c2cc224240fc" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573287 1074 "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,u\\,| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1068 17592186573163 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1069 "q-18057" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1071 "![](images/b9/b911126b4468e016afbab7faaf20223d0811827bb7f57cdefc2f1923cb7faf1b.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $C$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1072 #uuid "8c1db841-92ab-5a9b-b59a-bd03ce09afe3" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1074 "From the picture, we obtain $A(0,3)$ and $C(4,0).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=4i+0j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i)-(3j) \\\\  & =4i-3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1068 17592186573152 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1069 "q-138836" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{2}}g(x)}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1072 #uuid "8db2f83d-f6c6-583a-9190-f65543a5bd2c" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573289 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{({x}^{2}-2x-8)}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(\\frac{2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-16x-4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2)}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1068 17592186573199 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1069 "q-351023" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1072 #uuid "8f2aebee-176b-5447-94e6-cdb44a5559c2" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573290 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}x(\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{2})^{3})\\,({e}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-3{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6})-3x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1068 17592186573155 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1068 17592186573207 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1069 "q-275102" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1071 "Find the position vector of $R(-4,5)$ in terms of $i$ and $j.$\n\nThe position vector of $R$ is\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1072 #uuid "8f6ad506-8ae9-5d30-a511-9a3173b92b8c" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e1/e132f8e20ffb38c70ef435a560a584b25a6509486cc148636d873a9e0e94ea52.png)\n\nRemember that in general, if a point $R$ has coordinates $({r}_{x},{r}_{y}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}={r}_{x}i+{r}_{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $R$ has coordinates $(-4,5),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =(-4)\\cdot i+5\\cdot j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1068 17592186573153 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1069 "q-141914" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{3ab}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5b}=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1072 #uuid "90e80b30-af1b-594f-ba5d-4e938ac90b8f" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573292 1074 "We apply the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3ab}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3ab\\cdot 5b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{15a{b}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1068 17592186573229 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1068 17592186573231 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1069 "q-318162" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1071 "If $\\overset{\\to }{p}=⟨-1,5⟩,$ then $3\\overset{\\to }{p}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1072 #uuid "91b53c66-dd75-5cd1-95a9-a7a78fb6e36b" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\overset{\\to }{p} & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot (-1),\\,3\\cdot 5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1068 17592186573221 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1069 "q-1306" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1071 "Let $u=⟨7,4⟩$ and $v=⟨9,11⟩.$ Find $-2u+v.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1072 #uuid "933d30fa-71c5-533c-a1e8-a19050253145" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573294 1074 "To compute $-2u+v,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2u+v & =-2\\cdot ⟨7,4⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,-8⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1068 17592186573178 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1069 "q-1326" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1071 "If $u=⟨-3,-5⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $u?$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1072 #uuid "9c74dff8-a34c-5a12-a8c0-881272406626" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573295 1074 "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,u\\,| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1068 17592186573189 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1069 "q-130491" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1071 "Given that $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(D).$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1072 #uuid "9ebd673b-ab24-50e2-a8c2-fa7ddd43f728" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573296 1074 "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =((-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot 6)\\cdot (3\\cdot 1\\cdot -1) \\\\  & =(-12)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1068 17592186573247 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1069 "q-320964" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1071 "![](images/cf/cf7890789d1b80002ac2055fd61e8c8f58525f9bb1714bd77ff6e8542567e962.png)\n\nLet $A(-2,0)$ and $B(3,3).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1072 #uuid "a88ebacd-81cc-571b-8835-0aed7b200fdc" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-2i+0j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j)-(-2i+0j) \\\\  & =5i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1068 17592186573156 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1068 17592186573157 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1069 "q-275319" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1071 "If $a=5i-3j$ and $b=-i+5j,$ then $a+b=${{field-1}}$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1072 #uuid "aa045c10-f709-5818-81e4-f02ca81ca31a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573298 1074 "To compute $a+b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =(5i-3j)+(-i+5j) \\\\  & =5i-3j-i+5j \\\\  & =(5i-i)+(-3j+5j) \\\\  & =4i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1068 17592186573230 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1069 "q-70514" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1071 "![](images/64/64d06368521604c208208a9d72452282f1ee8e683257ed9cd0161e957e39173c.png)\n\nThe point $U$ is shown in the picture above. Find the coordinates of the corresponding position vector." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1072 #uuid "adab0e55-06f3-58c6-9e94-4deec9447e84" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1074 "For a point with coordinates $({a}_{x},{a}_{y}),$ the position vector is $⟨{a}_{x},{a}_{y}⟩.$\n\nThe point $U$ has coordinates $(3,2.5),$ so the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=⟨3,2.5⟩.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1068 17592186573196 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1069 "q-17616" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1071 "Let $a=⟨-2,7⟩.$ Find $|\\,a\\,|.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1072 #uuid "aee063d1-50f9-564e-a7e9-bfddcd038224" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573300 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{53}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1068 17592186573209 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1069 "q-182524" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1071 "$¬(R⇔¬S)≡$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1072 #uuid "babcf679-477c-52f6-b009-8d35e055441b" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573301 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(R⇔¬S)≡¬[(R⇒¬S)∧(¬S⇒R)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(R⇒¬S)∧(¬S⇒R)]≡¬[(¬R∨¬S)∧(¬¬S∨R)]\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬R∨¬S)∧(¬¬S∨R)]≡¬[(¬R∨¬S)∧(S∨R)]\n$$\n\nNow, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬R∨¬S)∧(S∨R)] & ≡¬(¬R∨¬S)∨¬(S∨R) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬R∧¬¬S)∨¬(S∨R)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we again simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬¬R∧¬¬S)∨¬(S∨R) & ≡(R∧S)∨¬(S∨R) \\\\  & ≡(R∧S)∨¬(R∨S)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1068 17592186573151 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1068 17592186573219 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1068 17592186573238 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1069 "q-215492" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1071 "Solve the absolute value inequality $|12z+7|\\ge 5.$\n\n$z\\le$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ or $\\,z$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1072 #uuid "bf74267c-d6a7-5491-a319-f2e29a265fc9" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573302 1074 "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $12z+7$ whose distance from zero is greater than or equal to $5.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z+7 & \\le -5 & \\text{or} &  & 12z+7 & \\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z+7 & \\le -5 &  &  & 12z+7 & \\ge 5 \\\\ 12z & \\le -12 &  &  & 12z & \\ge -2 \\\\ z & \\le -1 &  &  & z & \\ge -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & \\le -1 & \\text{or} &  & z & \\ge -\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1068 17592186573160 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1068 17592186573166 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1068 17592186573197 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1069 "q-182534" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1072 #uuid "c2988660-d9f8-5b55-910c-35b17be03f50" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573303 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1068 17592186573167 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1068 17592186573186 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1068 17592186573206 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1068 17592186573239 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1069 "q-182557" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A⇔¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1072 #uuid "c99a4f11-ff65-59dd-9169-cf64a0f04da0" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573304 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $P⇔Q$ is true if $P⇒Q$ **and** $Q⇒P.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $P⇔Q$ is true if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add columns for $¬A$ and $¬B:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $¬A⇔¬B.$ \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬B\\,$ | $\\,¬A⇔¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1068 17592186573202 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1068 17592186573224 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1068 17592186573228 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1068 17592186573244 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1069 "q-182582" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧¬B)⇔(A∨B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1072 #uuid "ca04b068-96be-5754-87a7-716e8d6e2a93" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573305 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $P⇔Q$ is true if $P⇒Q$ **and** $Q⇒P.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $P⇔Q$ is true if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add columns for $¬B,$ $A∧¬B,$ and $A∨B:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬B\\,$ | $\\,A∧¬B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $(A∧¬B)⇔(A∨B).$ \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬B\\,$ | $\\,A∧¬B\\,$ | $\\,A∨B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧¬B)⇔(A∨B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1068 17592186573193 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1068 17592186573222 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1068 17592186573227 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1068 17592186573242 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1069 "q-182579" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔(¬A∧B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1072 #uuid "cbd4644e-3a2a-55e7-829a-b73b408a214a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573306 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $P⇔Q$ is true if $P⇒Q$ **and** $Q⇒P.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $P⇔Q$ is true if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nFirst, we add columns for $¬A$ and $¬A∧B:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for  $A⇔(¬A∧B):$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔(¬A∧B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1068 17592186573187 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1069 "q-182569" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1071 "$\\,$ If you don't turn the volume up, then you won't hear what they're saying.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1072 #uuid "cf7bb594-de49-54f5-b420-2e5186a68020" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573307 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't turn the volume up.\n\n- $B:$ You won't hear what they're saying.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You turn the volume up.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Turn the volume up, or you won't hear what they're saying." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1068 17592186573234 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1069 "q-102734" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬A∨¬B)⇒¬A.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1072 #uuid "d17f8721-9970-54a5-a0a5-023397a542c4" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573308 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨¬B)⇒¬A≡¬(¬(¬A∨¬B))∨¬A.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬A∨¬B))∨¬A≡(¬A∨¬B)∨¬A.\n$$\n\nUsing the commutative and associative law, we have\n\n$$\n(¬A∨¬B)∨¬A≡(¬A∨¬A)∨¬B.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the idempotent law, we have\n\n$$\n(¬A∨¬A)∨¬B≡¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬A∨¬B.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1068 17592186573210 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1069 "q-152866" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1070 17592186045486 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1071 "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})?$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1072 #uuid "d1c19cde-3b7d-5f98-929b-d663820d03f1" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573309 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2}):$ \n\n![](images/ff/fffe2ba1124b8edd04902db5ad2b0945e5e04372ae0a0ed338c893f0ae417c58.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is $x\\in [2,6].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{2})$ is undefined." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1068 17592186573233 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1069 "q-125100" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1071 "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨4,-3⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨-1,2⟩$, then $2\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1072 #uuid "d424dcb1-177c-5053-898f-bbc5de5cd766" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =2\\cdot ⟨4,-3⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-6⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8+(-1),-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-1,-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1068 17592186573190 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1069 "q-1330" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1071 "Let $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨7,-1⟩.$ Find $|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}|.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1072 #uuid "de84f93a-9a95-5709-8b3a-1c7aaa638199" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1068 17592186573214 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1068 17592186573218 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1069 "q-318154" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1071 "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,4a-(3b-2c).$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$4a-(3b-2c)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1072 #uuid "df07f0e4-b8c0-55c8-a0ad-afd25f375b3a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573312 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a-(3b-2c) & =4\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(3\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-28 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1068 17592186573158 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1069 "q-130869" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}?$  \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1072 #uuid "e4359e6b-bf35-575a-ae91-b45c922f6599" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573313 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1068 17592186573172 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1069 "q-16096" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $-2$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $10$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=-4.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1072 #uuid "e56f348b-304a-594f-855c-44aa9e7abb11" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573314 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=-4} & =f(-4){g}^{′}(-4)+g(-4){f}^{′}(-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 10+(-7)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1068 17592186573212 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1068 17592186573232 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1069 "q-318160" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1071 "If $\\overset{\\to }{m}=⟨-6,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{n}=⟨5,-8⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{m}-\\overset{\\to }{n}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1072 #uuid "e78eae91-aaf3-5f8b-9296-2cbaa295070f" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{m}-\\overset{\\to }{n} & =⟨-6,2⟩-⟨5,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6-5,\\,2-(-8)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-11,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1068 17592186573204 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1069 "q-25774" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1071 "![](images/c1/c15de626535d55df85e756b8c3fffc9c4d50eb5b7e2b049eeec3f2ba6be2f2bb.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AD}.$ Find $BC$ if $AD=29\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD=6.5\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1072 #uuid "eaee2e36-f252-5a56-ba54-f7a90c1e698d" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1074 "Since $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AD}$, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =\\frac{1}{2}AD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =14.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by the segment addition postulate, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =BC+CD \\\\ 14.5 & =BC+6.5 \\\\ 14.5-6.5 & =BC \\\\ BC & =8\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1068 17592186573169 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1069 "q-182531" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1071 "If $A$ is false and $B$ is true, then $A⇔B$ is\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1072 #uuid "ec004b06-3312-5bf4-be49-682afc34bcbf" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573317 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A=\\text{F}$ and $B=\\text{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A⇔B) & =(\\text{F}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =\\text{F}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n|  |  |  |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1068 17592186573237 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1069 "q-14506" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1071 "A triangle has sides $j$, $k$, and $l.$ The sides $j$ and $l$ have lengths $4$ and $7$, respectively. If the angle $K$ between $j$ and $l$ has a measure of ${121}^{∘}$, what is the length of side $k?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1072 #uuid "edd7453d-0827-54b9-87f7-014fa66cf7ec" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573318 1074 "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/1f/1fde3029f6a50388ebd342dc4d15e9787567461b651c24cd6bd6843521b0465d.png)\n\nThe Law of Cosines states that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}={j}^{2}+{l}^{2}-2jl\\operatorname{cos}⁡K.\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nm∠K={121}^{∘},\\,j=4,\\,l=7.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the numbers into the formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{7}^{2}-2(4)(7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =16+49-56\\operatorname{cos}⁡{121}^{∘} \\\\  & =65-56(-0.515\\,03…) \\\\  & =65+28.842\\,13 \\\\  & =93.842\\,13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTaking the square root gives\n\n$$\nk=\\sqrt{93.842\\,13}=9.69\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1068 17592186573177 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1068 17592186573198 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1069 "q-318128" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1071 "If $a=⟨-4,6⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-5⟩$, then $a-b=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1072 #uuid "ee2b51f4-f377-5233-82c8-37f171607ed7" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573319 1074 "To compute $a-b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b & =⟨-4,6⟩-⟨3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4-3,6-(-5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1068 17592186573211 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1069 "q-125137" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1071 "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $r$ and points the same direction." 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1072 #uuid "efcda90e-97e9-5de4-ab6e-f342fb013418" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573320 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{r}{|\\,r\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,r\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $r$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{r}{|r|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot r \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1068 17592186573241 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1069 "q-18003" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1071 "![](images/94/944c91a9d428f3b84cd748d40da9178b494522a5b39777fe0b8e875f9733f537.png)\n\nLet $A(-1,2)$ and $B(-1,3).$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ in terms of $i$ and $j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1072 #uuid "f0302994-59bc-5fe6-beeb-f786801b16b0" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =0i+j \\\\  & =j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1068 17592186573243 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1068 17592186573246 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1069 "q-316790" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1071 "Find the position vector of $R(2,4)$ in terms of $i$ and $j.$\n\nThe position vector of $R$ is\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$j.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1072 #uuid "f1d56472-7ebb-55ed-bd03-c4b2a70fb522" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ec/ecc6f3682d17eb4878e43a24ada2d6989d73f34d44ce2e3adcf9e6dc201761d2.png)\n\nRemember that in general, if a point $R$ has coordinates $({r}_{x},{r}_{y}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}={r}_{x}i+{r}_{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $R$ has coordinates $(2,4),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =2\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1068 17592186573175 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1069 "q-182522" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1071 "$¬(B⇔A)≡$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1072 #uuid "f686088d-e105-5e19-956f-e84bb86ba487" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573323 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(B⇔A)≡¬[(B⇒A)∧(A⇒B)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(B⇒A)∧(A⇒B)]≡¬[(¬B∨A)∧(¬A∨B)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬B∨A)∧(¬A∨B)] & ≡¬(¬B∨A)∨¬(¬A∨B) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬B∧¬A)∨(¬¬A∧¬B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations and write down symbols in alphabetic order inside the parentheses using commutative laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬¬B∧¬A)∨(¬¬A∧¬B) & ≡(B∧¬A)∨(A∧¬B) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∧B)∨(A∧¬B)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1068 17592186573165 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1069 "q-215010" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1070 17592186045487 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1071 "What is $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ expressed in $\\text{mg}?$ Write your answer as a whole number.\n\n$0.9\\,\\text{g}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{mg}$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1072 #uuid "fa1ab2c6-2c4e-5cd2-a497-7648fbd76dfd" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573324 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\text{kg}$ | $1\\,\\text{kg}=1\\,000\\,\\text{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\text{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\text{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}=1\\,\\text{g}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}$ per $\\text{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\text{g}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.9\\,\\text{g} & =0.9\\,\\text{g}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\text{g}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\cancel{\\text{g}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}}{\\cancel{\\text{g}}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{mg} \\\\  & =900\\,\\text{mg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.9\\,\\text{g}$ is equivalent to $900\\,\\text{mg}.$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1068 17592186573223 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1069 "q-31950" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1071 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $H$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${H}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[H\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1072 #uuid "fc1b75b6-fb85-5b50-9394-b22e6f324813" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573325 1074 "We write down $H$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $H$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[H\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${H}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{H}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1068 17592186573249 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1069 "q-133220" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1070 17592186045485 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2ax{y}^{2}-4xy+15x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =b{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-4y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1072 #uuid "fcbd8ac8-2f1b-5dcb-9ac1-2425c8503e58" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573326 1074 "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2ax{y}^{2}-4xy+15x) \\\\  & =4axy-4x-0 \\\\  & =4axy-4x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(b{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-4y) \\\\  & =2bxy-4x-0 \\\\  & =2bxy-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4axy-4x &=2bxy-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy$, we see that $a=\\frac{b}{2}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)=-5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,-1) & =-5 \\\\ b(-1)^{2}(-1)-2(-1)^{2}-4(-1) & =-5 \\\\ -b-2+4 & =-5 \\\\ b & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na=\\frac{b}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573327 1058 17592186573332 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573327 1062 #uuid "02ef4fc2-6aff-5366-8724-8878c0dcf4a7" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573327 1063 17592186250284 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573328 1062 #uuid "049fc9f2-d3fc-5a75-bfdb-fec8013c9477" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573328 1063 17592186573281 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573329 1058 17592186573344 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573329 1062 #uuid "082272e6-9cdf-55aa-ad99-e25a5d09e819" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573329 1063 17592186573272 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573330 1058 17592186573342 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573330 1062 #uuid "0a8e6389-a5e8-5f3f-9c7c-f7d9c66a8d2a" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573330 1063 17592186482583 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573331 1058 17592186573369 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573331 1062 #uuid "142833f0-e6bd-5cd5-a5c3-41884f888f14" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573331 1063 17592186573256 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186